WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.760
Congratulations.

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:05.790
We've reached a branching
point in our lesson.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:08.370
Now a few different ideas
have been introduced,

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.320
so it's important to
orient ourselves here

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:13.170
before moving forward
in various directions.

00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:15.780
Now, what motivated this lesson?

00:00:15.780 --> 00:00:18.190
Well, we learned
about RSA encryption.

00:00:18.190 --> 00:00:21.750
And RSA depended on two things.

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:25.950
One, that prime
factorization is difficult.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:29.860
So if I multiply two big
primes together, P1 and P2,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:34.280
and I give you N, I can
trust, or feel secure,

00:00:34.280 --> 00:00:38.050
in knowing that you'll take a
long time to find those primes,

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:41.340
and maybe more
than your lifetime.

00:00:41.340 --> 00:00:46.740
Also, two, RSA requires that
those large primes I generated

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:50.420
was easy, meaning I could just
quickly generate a large prime.

00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:56.350
So let's return to the
first problem, difficulty

00:00:56.350 --> 00:00:57.480
of prime factorization.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.390
Well, what is it about
prime factorization, or just

00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:04.750
prime numbers themselves,
which make problems hard?

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:07.620
And to find out, we begin
with a core problem.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.440
Given x, is x
prime or composite,

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:12.410
which is a primality test.

00:01:12.410 --> 00:01:15.540
Now, we ended up building some
solutions to this problem.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.340
And in doing so, we
realized that this problem

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:21.250
was computationally expensive.

00:01:21.250 --> 00:01:24.600
That is, there was no instant
solution to this problem.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:27.850
As our input number
grew, the amount of time

00:01:27.850 --> 00:01:30.520
or the amount of steps
involved for our algorithm

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:33.670
to find the solution also grew.

00:01:33.670 --> 00:01:37.540
And how much it grew we
actually understand better now,

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:39.340
because we can predict
this search space

00:01:39.340 --> 00:01:41.250
using the prime number theorem.

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.720
Though, the real issue can be
thought of as a graph where,

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:47.850
on the vertical axis, we
have the number of steps

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:48.720
our algorithm takes.

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:50.750
So you could just
think of it as time.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.540
And on the x-axis,
is the input size.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:58.010
And as input size
increased, so did time.

00:01:58.010 --> 00:02:01.000
And the problem we had is
that the shape of this curve

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.400
is unacceptable.

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.890
Because in our case, once
N hit even 20 digits,

00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:09.520
we can no longer solve the
problem, in the worst case.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:11.510
And if we threw an
input hundreds of digits

00:02:11.510 --> 00:02:14.720
long at our algorithm, we can
all agree it would not work.

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:16.600
In our case, it would crash.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:19.510
So it's practically
impossible to check

00:02:19.510 --> 00:02:23.340
if large input is prime
using our current strategies.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.160
Now let's return to the
first point, factorization.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:30.600
Realize, based on our current
understanding in this lesson,

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:34.635
factorization has to be
harder than a primality test.

00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:39.870
That is, there are
more steps involved

00:02:39.870 --> 00:02:43.000
in finding the prime
factorization of some number,

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:45.720
versus just telling me
if a number is prime,

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:49.570
since, remember, factorization
requires us to find all

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:55.500
the prime factors for
some input, N. Whereas,

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:59.410
a primality test only requires
that we find one divisor.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:06.420
And a nice reminder of this is
that some users have actually

00:03:06.420 --> 00:03:09.680
turned these primality tests
into prime factorization

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.990
algorithms, simply
by repeating after it

00:03:12.990 --> 00:03:14.720
finds its first divisor.

00:03:14.720 --> 00:03:16.410
So the primality
test is just kind

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:20.445
of a sub routine inside the
main factorization algorithm.

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:27.920
So if we return to this graph,
a factorization algorithm

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.300
would be something like this.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.910
As input grows, our
factorization algorithm

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:37.590
would be above this
primality test line.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:39.340
And realize that,
in any situation,

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.350
we always have a
computational limit, that

00:03:41.350 --> 00:03:44.040
is the number of primitive
steps we can calculate,

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:49.070
which is based on our computing
power in any given situation

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.120
and the amount of time we had.

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:54.480
And you could think of
this as a horizontal line,

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:56.450
or a threshold on this graph.

00:03:56.450 --> 00:03:59.630
Anything above this
line is out of reach,

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:01.550
not feasible to solve.

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.170
And in this lesson,
we were limited

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:06.000
by the Rover's on-board
computer, which

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.510
was fairly slow, which is why
we couldn't run primality tests

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:12.190
on numbers with even 20 digits.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:15.480
However, even if we had,
say, 1,000 computers running

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:19.850
for a year, this would simply
just push this horizontal line

00:04:19.850 --> 00:04:23.100
up to some other threshold.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:26.250
And this would allow us to
run tests on larger numbers.

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:29.080
But as you can see, we
would always hit some limit

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:32.850
where the input is large enough
that we can no longer solve

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:33.790
the problems again.

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:41.040
Now, this leads to many
interesting question paths.

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.140
However, I'll identify the
two I'm going to explore next.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:49.560
First of all, so far, I
have not been very precise

00:04:49.560 --> 00:04:51.720
about these growth curves.

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.820
Though, it would be helpful
if, imagine, for any algorithm

00:04:55.820 --> 00:04:58.480
you give me, no matter
what it's trying to solve,

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:01.660
and no matter what machine
it's even running on,

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:07.040
we could draw some corresponding
growth curve on this graph,

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:11.240
simply by looking at the
description of the algorithm.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:15.250
If we could do this, we
could identify categories

00:05:15.250 --> 00:05:18.230
of certain growth
shapes which tell us

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:20.920
how difficult problem
will be to solve.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:23.220
And in this way, we could
understand an algorithm

00:05:23.220 --> 00:05:25.720
before we even run it.

00:05:25.720 --> 00:05:27.700
That's very important
to think about.

00:05:27.700 --> 00:05:30.360
And you could hand
me your algorithm

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.920
written on a piece of paper.

00:05:31.920 --> 00:05:32.890
And I could look at it.

00:05:32.890 --> 00:05:36.010
And I'll say, I know
this algorithm is not

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:38.540
solvable with the
input size needed.

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:42.410
And this leads us to a
lesson on time complexity,

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:46.722
an incredibly important
conceptual tool we will need.

00:05:46.722 --> 00:05:49.180
And if you've ever heard, oh,
this runs in polynomial time,

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:51.420
or exponential time,
these are terms

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:53.270
which come out of
time complexity.

00:05:53.270 --> 00:05:55.920
Next, many of you have
been wondering, well,

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:59.840
how are we generating these
random primes in practice,

00:05:59.840 --> 00:06:01.962
that second point
I made about RSA.

00:06:01.962 --> 00:06:03.670
Well, let's just walk
through it quickly.

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:07.800
To generate a large, random
prime number hundreds of digits

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:10.870
long, we need to follow
these instructions.

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.040
Generate a random number.

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:16.360
Test if it's prime.

00:06:16.360 --> 00:06:18.230
If it's prime, we're done.

00:06:18.230 --> 00:06:21.820
If not, try again,
until we hit a prime.

00:06:21.820 --> 00:06:23.440
Now in this
three-step procedure,

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:25.470
what's most important
is the second step,

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:26.520
test if it's prime.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:29.170
If we can't do that
step, this won't work.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:32.170
So how is this working today?

00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:35.810
Well, it's based on a subtle
alteration to our problem

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:38.490
definition, and
more importantly,

00:06:38.490 --> 00:06:40.760
the use of randomness.

00:06:40.760 --> 00:06:45.060
And this leads us to another
lesson on random algorithms.

00:06:45.060 --> 00:06:48.610
And now, finally, if there any
other lingering question paths

00:06:48.610 --> 00:06:50.790
you have, please
post them below.

00:06:50.790 --> 00:06:52.500
And we can plan lessons.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:54.440
For example, there are
some deeper mathematics

00:06:54.440 --> 00:06:57.450
we have yet to explore, in order
to speed up our existing trial

00:06:57.450 --> 00:06:59.620
division primality tests.

00:06:59.620 --> 00:07:01.720
And what else are
you thinking of?

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:03.800
Please share below.

